  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1241 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Mid-level ridging is in the process of \nbuilding across the Great Lakes this afternoon through tonight.\nAttendant surface high pressure in control through Thursday \nmorning. However, plenty of changes on the way Thursday \nafternoon through much of Friday as amplified troughing digs \nacross the nation's midsection. Two areas of low pressure to \nnote for Thursday, one diving in from the northwest out of \nSaskatchewan/Manitoba/Ontario, and the second, a more moisture \nrich wave trekking northeast out of the southern/central Plains.\nThese features largely phasing across the western Great Lakes \nby Thursday evening before racing east through the day Friday --\nto New England by 00z Saturday. Heights build behind this \nsystem with renewed surface high pressure looking to renew drier\nconditions and gradually moderating temperatures for the \nweekend into at least early next week. \n\nForecast Details: Primary focus through the forecast period revolves \naround Thursday afternoon through Friday with increasingly wet and \nwindy weather becoming the rule. PWs progged to increase to between \n1-1.4\" across the forecast area -- some +1 to 3 SD above the climo \nmean for mid-October. Should be a fairly widespread and steady rain \nfor much of the forecast area, especially beneficial for those in \nexpanding drought across the eastern U.P. Favored QPF amounts \nThursday afternoon through Friday afternoon range from 0.30-0.70\" -- \nhowever, 30-50% probabilities exist across portions of northwest \nlower MI for rain amounts to exceed an inch. Even some very low \n(<15%) probs for 2\"+ amounts over the same area. \n\nPrecip chances gradually diminish through Friday afternoon, although \na bit of lingering lake induced shower activity may persist through \nFriday evening/early overnight downwind of Lake Michigan & Superior. \nSuppose it's not entirely out of the question that if showers linger \nlate enough into Friday evening that a few inland/higher terrain \nlocations could mix in a few wet snowflakes given H8 temps falling \nto near -5C. Not the expectation at this point, and if it does\noccur, shouldn't cause any issues, but felt the chance was at \nleast worth mentioning. Thereafter, latest trends supporting \nmainly dry weather Saturday through at least the early to middle\nportions of next week with a slow moderating temperature trend\n(e.g highs Friday in the upper 40s to low 50s for most rise to \nthe low-mid 60s Sunday/Monday).",
